      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Measuring the unreachable"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "How tall is a tower you cannot climb? Trigonometry links the angles of a right triangle to the ratios of its sides, so we can measure from the ground."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "3-4-5 triangle",
          "cosec, sec, cot",
          "depend on the angle",
          "one ratio gives all",
          "opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse",
          "sin, cos <= 1",
          "sin, cos, tan",
          "tan = sin/cos"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Take a right triangle ABC, right-angled at B. For angle A, the side opposite is BC, the side adjacent is AB and the hypotenuse is AC.\nsin A = opposite by hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent by hypotenuse and tan A = opposite by adjacent.\nThe reciprocals are cosec A = 1 by sin A, sec A = 1 by cos A and cot A = 1 by tan A.\nAlso tan A = sin A by cos A, and cot A = cos A by sin A.\nThese ratios depend only on the angle and not on the size of the triangle, because similar triangles have proportional sides.\nExample: if sin A = 3 by 5 then the opposite side is 3 and the hypotenuse 5, so the adjacent side is 4 and cos A = 4/5, tan A = 3/4.\nThe values of sin A and cos A are never more than 1. The values of sec A and cosec A are at least 1.\nIf one ratio is known, you can find all the others by drawing the right triangle."
        }
